Good instinct — defense in depth here is cheap. Let me sketch what I'd actually ship.

## Proposal

A **prefix + exact-match allowlist**, with one opt-out env var for users who need more. Build it in a single helper called from `runPlugin`:

```go
// pluginEnv builds the child environment from the parent. We allowlist a
// small set of OS-plumbing and TUI-detection variables that virtually every
// program needs, plus prefixes we own (ENTIRE_*) or that are well-defined
// passthrough namespaces (LC_*, XDG_*). Anything else — credentials,
// tool-specific config, dev-loop debug flags — is dropped.
//
// Escape hatch: ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV="AWS_*,GIT_*,FOO" lets the user opt
// individual names or wildcard prefixes back in.
func pluginEnv(parent []string, extra ...string) []string { ... }
```

## The list

```go
// Exact names (case-insensitive on Windows).
var pluginEnvAllowed = map[string]struct{}{
    // POSIX basics
    "PATH": {}, "HOME": {}, "USER": {}, "LOGNAME": {},
    "SHELL": {}, "PWD": {}, "TMPDIR": {}, "TZ": {},

    // Locale
    "LANG": {}, "LANGUAGE": {},

    // Terminal / color
    "TERM": {}, "TERM_PROGRAM": {}, "TERM_PROGRAM_VERSION": {},
    "COLORTERM": {}, "NO_COLOR": {}, "FORCE_COLOR": {},
    "CLICOLOR": {}, "CLICOLOR_FORCE": {},

    // CI detection (broad — most tools can't detect CI any other way)
    "CI": {}, "GITHUB_ACTIONS": {}, "GITLAB_CI": {},
    "BUILDKITE": {}, "CIRCLECI": {}, "JENKINS_URL": {},
    "TEAMCITY_VERSION": {}, "TRAVIS": {},

    // Proxies (lowercase variants too — curl/git read both)
    "HTTP_PROXY": {}, "HTTPS_PROXY": {}, "NO_PROXY": {}, "ALL_PROXY": {},
    "http_proxy": {}, "https_proxy": {}, "no_proxy": {}, "all_proxy": {},

    // SSH agent (so plugins that fetch via git/ssh keep working)
    "SSH_AUTH_SOCK": {}, "SSH_CONNECTION": {},

    // Windows-essential
    "SYSTEMROOT": {}, "WINDIR": {}, "COMSPEC": {}, "PATHEXT": {},
    "APPDATA": {}, "LOCALAPPDATA": {}, "PROGRAMDATA": {},
    "PROGRAMFILES": {}, "PROGRAMFILES(X86)": {},
    "USERPROFILE": {}, "USERNAME": {}, "HOMEDRIVE": {}, "HOMEPATH": {},

    // Already documented in our CLAUDE.md
    "ACCESSIBLE": {},
}

var pluginEnvPrefixes = []string{
    "ENTIRE_",  // our namespace — we already inject CLI_VERSION, REPO_ROOT
    "LC_",      // locale family
    "XDG_",     // config-dir conventions
}
```

## What this drops (deliberately)

- **Cloud creds**: `AWS_*`, `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, `AZURE_*`
- **Other CLI tokens**: `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GITLAB_TOKEN`, `NPM_TOKEN`, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`
- **DB DSNs**: `DATABASE_URL`, `*_DSN`
- **Git config**: `GIT_*` — most are config but a few (`GIT_ASKPASS`, `GIT_SSH_COMMAND`) carry credential paths. Drop the whole family by default; let users opt in via `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV=GIT_*` if they have a plugin that needs it.
- **Editor/pager**: `EDITOR`, `VISUAL`, `PAGER` — debatable; I'd start without them and add if a real plugin asks.

## Escape hatches

1. **`ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV`** — comma-separated names or `PREFIX_*` wildcards. Gives the user a one-line override without us having to ship a release: `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV='AWS_*,EDITOR' entire deploy ...`.
2. **`ENTIRE_*` namespace** — for our own future contracts (auth callbacks, telemetry, repo root). Already partially done.
3. **Future plugin manifest** — `entire-foo` could ship a sidecar declaring what it needs. Don't build this yet; revisit when a real plugin asks.

## Tradeoffs to call out before shipping

- **Behavior divergence from kubectl/gh.** Worth a one-paragraph note in `external-commands.md` explaining the choice ("we filter; here's how to opt back in"). Reviewers will ask.
- **Plugin authors will hit this.** First time someone writes `entire-deploy` and `AWS_PROFILE` is missing, they'll be confused. Make sure the error path is good — when a plugin exits non-zero we could (separately, as a follow-up) suggest checking `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV` if their stderr mentions a missing env var. Probably overkill though.
- **Test surface**: a single table-driven test in `plugin_test.go` covering each category (allowed exact, allowed prefix, dropped credential, opt-in via `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_ENV`).

Want me to implement this, or is this just exploring? If implementing, I'd structure it as: new `pluginEnv` function in `plugin.go`, table-driven test, and an `external-commands.md` section documenting the allowlist + escape hatch.

